# Line endings, pinned. AC-13 (S30) depends on this.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. The blobs stored in the repository are LF either
# way, so nothing looks wrong in a diff, and a working tree whose files were
# written by the generators looks correct too.
#
# A FRESH CLONE does not. Measured on 2026-09-08: `db/seed/04_samples.sql` came
# out of a clone 5,160 bytes larger than the file the generator writes, and
# `data/synthetic/portfolio.csv` 201 bytes larger, one byte per line in each.
#
# The application does not care. Postgres and Python's csv reader both accept
# either ending. What breaks is the property S30 exists to protect: that the
# committed artefacts can be regenerated and compared. On a clean clone every
# generated file differed from its generator's output in every line, so
# `tests/prep/test_clean_machine.py` failed and a real drift would have been
# invisible inside the noise.
#
# Every generator in this repository writes LF explicitly (`newline="\n"` in the
# Python emitters, `writeFileSync` in the TypeScript ones). This makes the
# checkout agree with them.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The artefacts the clean-machine tests compare byte for byte. Declared
# individually as well, so the rule survives someone relaxing the line above.
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.csv  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf

# Not text. Left untouched, and never diffed as lines.
*.pmtiles binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.png     binary
*.ico     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
